Step 1: Create Lambda execution role
Why? Lambda needs an IAM role to execute and write its execution logs to CloudWatch.
cat <<'EOF' > trust-policy.json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {
      "Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com"
    },
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
  }]
}
EOF


Create the role:
aws iam create-role \
  --role-name training-chat-lambda-role \
  --assume-role-policy-document file://trust-policy.json


Attach CloudWatch logging permissions:
aws iam attach-role-policy \
  --role-name training-chat-lambda-role \
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole


Step 2: Create Lambda function
Why? Lambda acts as our backend processing engine. It receives the user's message, counts words and letters, and prepares a response.

Create the Python code:
cat <<'EOF' > lambda_function.py
import json

def lambda_handler(event, context):

    body = json.loads(event.get('body') or '{}')
    message = body.get('message', '')

    words = len(message.split())
    letters = sum(1 for char in message if char.isalpha())

    response = {
        "words": words,
        "letters": letters,
        "reply": f"Your message contains {words} words and {letters} letters."
    }

    return {
        "statusCode": 200,
        "headers": {
            "Content-Type": "application/json"
        },
        "body": json.dumps(response)
    }
EOF


Create a deployment package:
zip function.zip lambda_function.py


Create the Lambda function:
ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)

ROLE_ARN="arn:aws:iam::$ACCOUNT_ID:role/training-chat-lambda-role"

sleep 10

aws lambda create-function \
  --function-name training-chat-engine \
  --runtime python3.13 \
  --role "$ROLE_ARN" \
  --handler lambda_function.lambda_handler \
  --zip-file fileb://function.zip \
  --timeout 15 \
  --memory-size 128 \
  --region ap-south-1


Step 3: Create API Gateway HTTP API
Why? API Gateway provides an HTTP endpoint through which the frontend can communicate with Lambda. We will use an HTTP API because it is simple and cost-effective for this demonstration.

Create the API:
API_ID=$(aws apigatewayv2 create-api \
  --name training-chat-api \
  --protocol-type HTTP \
  --cors-configuration '{"AllowOrigins":["*"],"AllowMethods":["POST","OPTIONS"],"AllowHeaders":["content-type"]}' \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --query 'ApiId' \
  --output text)

echo $API_ID


Step 4: Integrate API Gateway with Lambda
Why? This establishes the connection between API Gateway and our Lambda backend. When API Gateway receives a request, it forwards the request to Lambda.

Get Lambda ARN:
LAMBDA_ARN=$(aws lambda get-function \
  --function-name training-chat-engine \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --query 'Configuration.FunctionArn' \
  --output text)


Create the integration:
INTEGRATION_ID=$(aws apigatewayv2 create-integration \
  --api-id "$API_ID" \
  --integration-type AWS_PROXY \
  --integration-uri "$LAMBDA_ARN" \
  --payload-format-version 2.0 \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --query 'IntegrationId' \
  --output text)

echo $INTEGRATION_ID


Step 5: Create API route
Why? A route defines the HTTP method and path through which users access the backend functionality.

We will create a POST endpoint:
POST /chat


Create the route:
aws apigatewayv2 create-route \
  --api-id "$API_ID" \
  --route-key 'POST /chat' \
  --target "integrations/$INTEGRATION_ID" \
  --region ap-south-1


Step 6: Allow API Gateway to invoke Lambda
Why? API Gateway needs explicit permission to invoke our Lambda function.
aws lambda add-permission \
  --function-name training-chat-engine \
  --statement-id AllowAPIGatewayInvocation \
  --action lambda:InvokeFunction \
  --principal apigateway.amazonaws.com \
  --source-arn "arn:aws:execute-api:ap-south-1:$ACCOUNT_ID:$API_ID/*/POST/chat" \
  --region ap-south-1


Step 7: Deploy the API
Why? Deployment makes the API route accessible through an HTTPS endpoint.
aws apigatewayv2 create-stage \
  --api-id "$API_ID" \
  --stage-name '$default' \
  --auto-deploy \
  --region ap-south-1


Get the API URL:
API_URL=$(aws apigatewayv2 get-api \
  --api-id "$API_ID" \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --query 'ApiEndpoint' \
  --output text)

echo $API_URL


Your API endpoint will look similar to:
https://xxxxxxxx.execute-api.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com


The actual chat endpoint is:
https://xxxxxxxx.execute-api.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/chat


Step 8: Test API Gateway and Lambda independently
Why? Before connecting the frontend, we should verify that the backend integration works.
curl -X POST "$API_URL/chat" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"message":"Welcome to AWS serverless training"}'


Expected response:
{
  "words": 5,
  "letters": 31,
  "reply": "Your message contains 5 words and 31 letters."
}


If this works, API Gateway and Lambda are successfully integrated.

5. Build the Chat Frontend
Step 9: Create chat.html
Why? This is the user interface of our chat application. It accepts messages, sends them to API Gateway using JavaScript fetch(), and displays the response returned by Lambda.

Create the frontend:
cat <<'EOF' > chat.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>AWS Serverless Chat</title>
<style>
* { box-sizing: border-box; }

body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #111827, #1e3a5f);
    height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    color: white;
}

.chat {
    width: 95%;
    max-width: 600px;
    height: 85vh;
    background: #ffffff;
    border-radius: 18px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow: hidden;
    color: #1f2937;
    box-shadow: 0 15px 50px #0005;
}

.header {
    background: #172554;
    color: white;
    padding: 22px;
}

.header h2 { margin: 0 0 6px; }
.header p { margin: 0; font-size: 13px; color: #bfdbfe; }

.messages {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 20px;
    overflow-y: auto;
    background: #f8fafc;
}

.message {
    padding: 13px 16px;
    border-radius: 14px;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    max-width: 85%;
    line-height: 1.5;
    font-size: 14px;
}

.bot {
    background: #e2e8f0;
    align-self: flex-start;
}

.user {
    background: #2563eb;
    color: white;
    margin-left: auto;
}

.input-area {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    padding: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid #e5e7eb;
}

input {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 14px;
    border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
    border-radius: 10px;
    outline: none;
    font-size: 14px;
}

button {
    background: #2563eb;
    color: white;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0 22px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: bold;
}

button:disabled { opacity: 0.5; }

</style>
</head>

<body>

<div class="chat">
    <div class="header">
        <h2>Cloud Chat Assistant</h2>
        <p>Powered by AWS API Gateway and Lambda</p>
    </div>

    <div class="messages" id="messages">
        <div class="message bot">
            Hello! Welcome to AWS Serverless Chat.
            Send me a message and I will count your words and letters.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="input-area">
        <input id="messageInput"
               placeholder="Type your message here..."
               onkeydown="if(event.key==='Enter') sendMessage()">
        <button id="sendButton" onclick="sendMessage()">Send</button>
    </div>
</div>

<script>
const API_URL = "API_ENDPOINT_PLACEHOLDER";

function addMessage(text, type) {
    const box = document.getElementById("messages");
    const msg = document.createElement("div");
    msg.className = "message " + type;
    msg.textContent = text;
    box.appendChild(msg);
    box.scrollTop = box.scrollHeight;
}

async function sendMessage() {
    const input = document.getElementById("messageInput");
    const button = document.getElementById("sendButton");
    const message = input.value.trim();

    if (!message) return;

    addMessage(message, "user");
    input.value = "";
    button.disabled = true;

    try {
        const response = await fetch(API_URL + "/chat", {
            method: "POST",
            headers: {
                "Content-Type": "application/json"
            },
            body: JSON.stringify({ message: message })
        });

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("API request failed: " + response.status);
        }

        const data = await response.json();
        addMessage(data.reply, "bot");

    } catch (error) {
        addMessage("Sorry, something went wrong. Please try again.", "bot");
        console.error(error);
    } finally {
        button.disabled = false;
        input.focus();
    }
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
EOF


Replace the API placeholder with your actual API endpoint:
sed -i '' "s|API_ENDPOINT_PLACEHOLDER|$API_URL|" chat.html


Step 10: Upload chat.html to S3
Why? S3 will host the frontend application. CloudFront will distribute the chat interface to users.
aws s3 cp chat.html s3://$BUCKET/chat.html \
  --content-type text/html


Since we previously configured S3 upload events to trigger Lambda, uploading chat.html will also automatically trigger the CloudFront invalidation function.
Step 11: Access the Chat Application
Get your CloudFront domain:
DOMAIN=$(aws cloudfront get-distribution \
  --id $DIST_ID \
  --query 'Distribution.DomainName' \
  --output text)

echo "https://$DOMAIN/chat.html"
